(
    version: 1,
    id: "m12",
    title: "Torque and Tipping",
    chapter: 4,
    concepts: ["Gear ratios", "Centre of mass"],
    world: (id: "ramp_lab", variant: Some("ramp_25")),
    robot: Build(max_parts: 120),
    time_limit_s: 25.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.7, 1.0),
    brief_md: r#"
## Design a robot that climbs the steep ramp

Build your own robot (up to **120 parts**) and write its program. It must reach the **top zone of
the 25 degree ramp within 20 seconds** without ever tilting more than **45 degrees**.

A steep ramp asks for more torque from the wheels and more care with the **centre of mass**: if it
is too high or too far back, the robot tips over backwards. Gears trade speed for torque: a small
gear driving a big one gives a robot that is slower but pulls harder.

Build the robot in the Builder, save it, then pick it for this mission. The ramp's surface is plastic;
rubber tyres grip it.
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "How hard must the wheels pull along the 25 degree ramp, in newtons, to hold a robot of 1 kg still?",
        unit: "N",
        measured: "hold_force_n",
    )),
    criteria: [FinishWithin(zone: "top_25", within_s: 20.0), NoTip(max_tilt_rad: 0.7853981633974483), MaxParts(n: 120)],
    stars: (
        (text: "Reach the top in under 10 seconds", criteria: [FinishWithin(zone: "top_25", within_s: 10.0)]),
        (text: "Use fewer than 60 parts", criteria: [FinishWithin(zone: "top_25", within_s: 10.0), MaxParts(n: 60)]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Start from the Rover. Does it climb at full power? Watch the tilt in the plotter.",
        "Put heavy parts (the hub, the motors) low and near the wheels. A long, low robot is harder to tip than a short, tall one.",
        "A small gear on the motor driving a big gear on the wheel axle gives more torque and less speed. Use the position of the wheels to keep the load over the drive axle.",
    ),
)
